    {
      "id": "026000-m01",
      "anchor": "Cauchy's theorem says that a holomorphic function has integral zero around the boundary of a filled triangle",
      "source_ids": [
        "nt-mit-complex-18-04"
      ],
      "source_locator": "Cauchy-Goursat theorem for triangles",
      "audit_status": "reading_reference_recorded"
    },
    {
      "id": "026000-m02",
      "anchor": "two pi i times f of a equals the integral around C of f of z divided by z minus a.",
      "source_ids": [
        "nt-mit-complex-18-04"
      ],
      "source_locator": "Cauchy integral formula",
      "audit_status": "reading_reference_recorded"
    },
    {
      "id": "026000-m03",
      "anchor": "Every holomorphic function therefore equals a convergent power series locally.",
      "source_ids": [
        "nt-mit-complex-18-112"
      ],
      "source_locator": "Cauchy formula and Taylor expansions",
      "audit_status": "reading_reference_recorded"
    },
    {
      "id": "026000-m04",
      "anchor": "This proves that zeros of a nonzero holomorphic function are isolated.",
      "source_ids": [
        "nt-mit-complex-18-112"
      ],
      "source_locator": "Isolated zeros and identity theorem",
      "audit_status": "reading_reference_recorded"
    },
    {
      "id": "026000-m05",
      "anchor": "The coefficient of that reciprocal term has a special name: the residue.",
      "source_ids": [
